  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Declare rigor FULL. Confirm task 144 has merged into this branch; re-derive every anchor above against the post-144 code.</step>
    <step order="1">Live read-only checks in dev (record in notes/task-133-provision-creator-lockout.md): the value of `organization.sharetopreviousowneronassign`; the Assign cascade configuration of every 1:N relationship on each provisionable root (background (d)); for every creation path of every provisionable root after 144 (CreateProjectWizard, SummarizeFilesDialog, the matter and work-assignment wizards, and any BFF/Office/Assistant server-side create), whether `createdby` is the human user; every active `sprk_fieldmappingrule` (or other create-time default) whose target field is `sprk_containerid` on a provisionable root (a create-time writer would break the "Step 7 is the sole writer" premise); census of rows with `sprk_issecure eq true` owned by the secure owner team, split by empty vs non-empty `sprk_containerid` (the empty ones are today's stranded rows the resume path will serve; each non-empty one must be matched to a container provisioning created).</step>
    <step order="2">Live proof of share-first on a throwaway secure project with an existing non-admin test user: grant the owner a POA share, reassign to the secure owner team, read the shares back. Choose share-first or post-move grant from the evidence.</step>
    <step order="3">Implement: pre-mutation WhoAmI; share (share-first or post-move per step 2); post-move complete read-back; verified compensation; the resume branch of the Step-4 marker; named colleagues after the creator is proven.</step>
    <step order="4">Correct every false statement listed in the background (server comments incl. `:29-31`, `:61-64`, `:860-864`/`:876-880`, `:962-967`, `:996-1015`; ProblemDetails details; the RecordContainerResolver route text; the wizard comment) and re-align the client copy in provisioningService.ts (incl. `:161-167`) to the new reason codes and states; add the wizard's "Try securing again" action for the retryable states.</step>
    <step order="5">Tests per the acceptance criteria.</step>
    <step order="6">Add the failure-states-and-recovery section to SECURE-PROJECT-ENVIRONMENT-SETUP.md: each reasonCode, the record's state after it, and who can recover how (self-service retry vs administrator).</step>
    <step order="7">Build, BFF unit + integration + arch suites, Spaarke.UI.Components jest for the provisioning client, publish-size and CVE checks per CLAUDE.md §10.</step>
    <step order="8">Run the manual live gate and record results (ids truncated to 8 chars) in notes/task-133-provision-creator-lockout.md.</step>
    <step order="9">Update TASK-INDEX.md: this task's status cell to `✅ [done]`.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>`organization.sharetopreviousowneronassign` is true in dev: STOP. Every assign (including compensation) would then add a platform-issued share, and the post-move read-back would see a share this code did not grant. Present the setting and its effect per CLAUDE.md §6 (task 144 carries the same stop).</trigger>
    <trigger>Any creation path of a provisionable root (wizard host, SummarizeFilesDialog, or a BFF/Office/Assistant server-side create after task 144 widens provisioning to matters and work assignments) creates the row through an application identity (so `createdby` is an application user): `createdby` cannot be the persisted creator for those rows. STOP and bring the choice (a new server-stamped creator column with a CLAUDE.md §11 justification, `createdonbehalfby`, or another existing column) to the owner, with a recommendation, rather than sharing on resume to whoever calls. Recommendation to carry: the existing `createdby` for human-created rows plus refusal (own reasonCode) for app-created rows, until the owner decides.</trigger>
    <trigger>The live census finds a secure-team-owned row whose `sprk_containerid` is non-empty but is NOT a container provisioning created (e.g. a pre-task-076 business-unit cascade value), OR finds any create-time writer of `sprk_containerid` on a provisionable root (e.g. an active field-mapping rule): the "team-owned + container recorded = provisioned" marker would 409 such a row forever. STOP and list the rows/rules; do not widen the resume condition on a guess.</trigger>
    <trigger>Any provisionable root's 1:N relationship cascades Assign (background (d)): the forward move and the compensating move would re-own children, and compensation cannot restore each child's own previous owner. STOP and present per CLAUDE.md §6 — options: (a) snapshot and restore each re-owned child's owner in compensation; (b) no compensation for that root type (resume only); (c) coordinate with task 148, which owns child ownership at provisioning. Recommendation: (c) then (a).</trigger>
    <trigger>The live proof shows neither share-first nor an immediate post-move grant can be made reliable (e.g. GrantAccess on a team-owned secure record is refused for the app user): the design premise fails — STOP and escalate with the evidence.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>New surface in this task: (1) up to two new `sdap.provision.*` reason codes (creator_share_failed_resumable; resume refused for an unusable createdby) beside the eleven at `ProvisionProjectEndpoint.cs:141-156`; (2) a "Try securing again" action in the wizard's provisioning-failure state. Overlap checked: the only in-product retry of a BFF mutation in this wizard folder is `CloseProjectDialog.tsx:347` (`handleRetry`, close-project); grep of src for `provisionSecureProject` / `provision-project` callers finds only `CreateProjectWizard.tsx:691` (via `provisioningService.ts:293`) — no existing retry of provisioning anywhere. Task 150 adds a record-level secure surface later, which is a different moment (on the record form), not the wizard's failure state. No new column: the resume creator is `createdby`.</existing>
    <extension>Reason codes: extend the existing `sdap.provision.*` set — an existing code cannot be reused because `creator_share_failed` will mean "ownership and shares are back, retry" and the double-failure state means the opposite, and the client must tell them apart. Retry: extend the existing wizard failure state with one action following the CloseProjectDialog pattern and the existing `provisionSecureProject` call; no new service or component.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Without the distinct code, the client cannot tell "you can retry now" from "an administrator must finish this" and must show one message for both — one of them false. Without the retry action, the server's "the same caller may retry" is unactionable: the wizard calls provisioning once (`CreateProjectWizard.tsx:707-713`), so the user is left with a secure-flagged project that refuses uploads (`RecordContainerResolver.cs:244-266`) and no way to finish securing it.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

